      <div class="msv__content">
        <p>Yes, you can work while on a Spanish student visa, but there are strict rules. Spain allows international students to work part-time to support themselves financially, but employment is limited to specific hours and conditions to ensure studies remain your priority.</p>
<h2>Working Hours Allowed on a Spanish Student Visa</h2>
<p>As an international student in Spain, you&#x27;re permitted to work up to 20 hours per week during the academic year. During official university holidays (summer, Christmas, Easter breaks), you can work full-time, up to 40 hours per week. This flexibility allows you to earn during intensive periods while maintaining your academic commitments.</p>
<p>If your studies are part-time (less than 20 hours per week), you have more flexibility to work additional hours, but total work and study combined cannot exceed 40 hours weekly. Your university must agree this arrangement is compatible with your academic progress.</p>
<h2>Types of Employment You Can Take</h2>
<p>Part-Time Jobs and Services</p>
<p>Most part-time jobs are available to student visa holders: retail, hospitality, language tutoring, administrative work, and freelance services. Many students work in bars, restaurants, shops, or as tutors for English language learners—sectors that actively hire students.</p>
<p>University Employment</p>
<p>Working at your university or college is often the easiest path. Many Spanish universities employ international students as teaching assistants, library staff, campus ambassadors, or support roles. These positions are typically flexible with academic schedules and count toward your work hours.</p>
<p>Self-Employment and Freelancing</p>
<p>Freelancing (remote work for companies outside Spain, or local freelance services) is technically permitted, but you must register as self-employed (autónomo) and pay Spanish social contributions. This is more complex than regular employment and involves administrative and tax obligations.</p>
<p>Need guidance on work arrangements while studying?</p>
<p>Understanding your exact work rights depends on your specific visa conditions and university program. A consultation can clarify what&#x27;s permitted in your situation.</p>
<div class="msv__cta-box">
  <p style="margin-bottom:1.2rem;font-size:0.97rem;">Book a Free Consultation and find the right visa route for your situation.</p>
  <a href="https://dashboard.platinumlegalspain.com/student-visa" class="msv__btn msv__btn--primary">Book a Free Consultation</a>
</div>
<h2>What Jobs Are Prohibited or Restricted</h2>
<p>You cannot work in sectors classified as dangerous or unhealthy for students (hazardous industrial work, mining, etc.). Additionally, some positions requiring specific Spanish qualifications or professional certifications are off-limits. Management positions and roles that would typically require relocation or full-time commitment are also not suited to student visa holders.</p>
<h2>Reporting Your Employment to Immigration</h2>
<p>You don&#x27;t need separate permission to work once your student visa is approved, but your employer must follow Spanish employment law and register you for social security. Keep documentation of your employment contract and hours—immigration or your university may request proof that you&#x27;re not exceeding limits.</p>
<h2>Impact on Your Student Visa Status</h2>
<p>Working part-time within the allowed hours does not jeopardize your student visa. However, if you work excessively (exceeding 20 hours weekly during the term), fail to demonstrate academic progress, or prioritize work over studies, your visa can be revoked. Universities monitor this through attendance and grades.</p>
<h2>Tax and Social Security Obligations</h2>
<p>Employed students in Spain pay income tax (though lower earners may qualify for exemptions) and contribute to the Spanish social security system through payroll deductions. Your employer handles these calculations. If you&#x27;re self-employed, you must register and pay contributions monthly—a significant expense worth considering.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Work and Study Balance</h2>
<p>Many students successfully balance part-time work with full-time studies. Choose flexible employers that understand student schedules, prioritize campus or service jobs when possible, and communicate with your academic advisor about your work plans. Earning €500-800 monthly is realistic for 20-hour-per-week part-time work in Spanish cities.</p>
